{"meta":{"course":"ai-ethics","chapter":"06","section":"02","chapter_title":"AI in Education Ethics","title":"Module 2: Surveillance, Dark Patterns, and Learner Autonomy","description":"Distinguish monitoring from surveillance, identify dark patterns specific to learner-facing AI (variable-ratio rewards, loss aversion, manufactured peer presence), and use self-determination theory to anchor a learner-autonomy-respecting design.","learning_objectives":[{"id":"ai-ethics-06-02-lo-01","statement":"State three properties (aggregation depth, asymmetry of access, inability to opt out) that distinguish surveillance from monitoring"},{"id":"ai-ethics-06-02-lo-02","statement":"Recognize five dark patterns specific to learner-facing AI and the developmental concerns they exploit"},{"id":"ai-ethics-06-02-lo-03","statement":"Apply self-determination theory's autonomy-competence-relatedness frame to evaluate an educational AI deployment"},{"id":"ai-ethics-06-02-lo-04","statement":"Run a seven-step pre-deployment audit that combines legal floor, monitoring vs. surveillance, engagement mechanics, autonomy, and procedural due process"}],"concept_ids":["educational_surveillance","learner_autonomy","self_determination_theory","engagement_dark_patterns","manufactured_social_presence"],"prereq_section_ids":["ai-ethics/06/01"],"practice_ids":["edu_monitor_vs_surveil","edu_engagement_motivation","edu_dark_pattern_recognize","edu_autonomy_design"],"estimated_time_minutes":13,"order":2,"lo_practice_map":{"ai-ethics-06-02-lo-01":["edu_monitor_vs_surveil"],"ai-ethics-06-02-lo-02":["edu_dark_pattern_recognize"],"ai-ethics-06-02-lo-03":["edu_autonomy_design","edu_engagement_motivation"],"ai-ethics-06-02-lo-04":["edu_autonomy_design"]},"alignment_method":"deterministic lexical mapping v2 with direct-evidence fallback; instructor review required"},"content":"# Surveillance, Dark Patterns, and Learner Autonomy\n\n<figure-block id=\"section-anchor-ai-ethics-06-02\" caption=\"Learning map for Module 2: Surveillance, Dark Patterns, and Learner Autonomy: principle, evidence, and transfer.\" purpose=\"organizational\" source=\"ALGET original instructional diagram\" license=\"Project-authored; repository license\">\n  <img src=\"/course-art/section-anchors/ai-ethics/06-02.svg\" alt=\"Learning map for Module 2: Surveillance, Dark Patterns, and Learner Autonomy, connecting educational surveillance, learner autonomy, self determination theory to evidence and transfer.\" loading=\"lazy\" decoding=\"async\" />\n</figure-block>\n\n\n> **\"The student is the one party in the educational data economy who is least able to say no.\"**\n> - paraphrasing the through-line of Selwyn, *Distrusting Educational Technology*\n\n---\n\n## Opening: The Proctoring Case That Reached a Federal Court\n\nIn August 2022, a federal judge in *Ogletree v. Cleveland State University* ruled that a remote-proctoring \"room scan,\" in which a student was required to pan a webcam around his private bedroom before an online chemistry exam, was an unreasonable search under the Fourth Amendment. The university had argued the scan was routine, consented to, and necessary to preserve academic integrity. The court disagreed: the scan was a government search of a private home, the student's \"consent\" was coerced by the requirement to take the exam, and less intrusive alternatives existed.\n\nThe case is worth holding onto because it compresses the entire module into one event. A tool marketed as benign analytics (\"we just verify the testing environment\") turned out to be surveillance; the consent was not real consent because the context was coercive; and the harm was invisible until someone with standing forced it into the open. Most educational AI never reaches a courtroom, which is precisely the problem. The defaults ship, the metrics improve, and no one with the student's interest in mind is in the room when the design decisions are made.\n\nThis module gives you the vocabulary and the workflow to be that person. It draws on Selwyn's *Distrusting Educational Technology*, Williamson's work on the data-fication of schooling, the National Academies *Governance of Artificial Intelligence* report, the Norwegian Consumer Council's *Deceived by Design*, and ongoing FTC enforcement (notably the 2023 Edmodo action) against deceptive ed-tech.\n\n---\n\n## Learning Objectives\n\nBy the end of this section, you will be able to:\n\n1. **Distinguish** surveillance from monitoring using three operational tests: aggregation depth, asymmetry of access, and inability to opt out.\n2. **Identify** five dark patterns specific to learner-facing AI and name the developmental vulnerability each one exploits.\n3. **Apply** self-determination theory's autonomy-competence-relatedness frame to evaluate an educational AI deployment.\n4. **Run** a seven-step pre-deployment audit and defend a redesign decision to a parent or learner a year later.\n\n---\n\n## What Educational AI Inherits From Persuasive-Tech Design\n\nThe same dark patterns that consumer-tech regulators are now prosecuting in the EU under the Digital Services Act show up regularly in educational AI products: streaks, badges, notification cadences, engagement-optimized recommendations, and surveillance dashboards branded as analytics. The educational frame allows persuasion that consumer products would not. The premise is that the persuasion is for the learner's benefit, so the safeguards that apply to manipulating adults can be relaxed when manipulating children \"for their own good.\"\n\nThat premise is wrong twice. It overestimates how well designers can predict what is good for any given learner, and it underestimates how durable the habits cultivated by design are. A streak mechanic does not just shape tonight's study session; it trains a reward circuit the learner carries forward.\n\n---\n\n## Educational Surveillance: From Formative Monitoring to Pervasive Recording\n\nA formative-assessment system that records which problems a student got wrong is monitoring. A platform that records keystroke timing, eye-gaze, webcam-detected emotion, and conversation-window time on every page is surveillance. The line between them is contested but real, and you can operationalize it with three tests.\n\n**Aggregation depth.** Monitoring tracks specific learning-relevant signals. Surveillance combines streams (behavior + biometric + social + temporal) into profiles that exceed any single signal's purpose. The danger is emergent: keystroke cadence alone is innocuous, but keystroke cadence plus webcam affect plus time-of-day can infer mental-health state that no one consented to disclose.\n\n**Asymmetry of access.** In monitoring, the student can usually see what is recorded. In surveillance, the dashboard is for the institution; the student is rarely shown their own profile. Asymmetry is the signature of a power imbalance: the watched party cannot audit the watcher.\n\n**Inability to opt out.** Monitoring tied to a specific assignment is bounded. Surveillance that runs whenever the student is logged in cannot be declined without leaving the institution. This is why \"consent\" in schools is so fragile: refusing the tool means refusing the course.\n\nOnline proctoring platforms operationalize all three properties at once, which is why *Ogletree* (above) was decided the way it was. The FTC's 2023 enforcement against Edmodo for COPPA and FTC Act violations, which alleged that the company conditioned classroom use on children's data and pushed compliance burdens onto teachers, marks a regulatory turn from \"notice\" toward \"you may not collect this at all.\"\n\nA defensible educational AI deployment chooses **monitoring rather than surveillance**: bounded scope, learner-visible records, real opt-out for non-essential streams.\n\n<inline-check question=\"Keystroke cadence is logged, webcam affect is logged, and time-of-day is logged. Each stream alone is innocuous. Which surveillance test does combining them most directly trip?\" explanation=\"Aggregation depth is the test about combining streams into profiles that exceed any single signal's purpose. Keystroke plus affect plus time-of-day can infer mental-health state no one consented to disclose; the danger is emergent from the combination.\" options='[{\"text\":\"Aggregation depth\",\"correct\":true},{\"text\":\"Asymmetry of access\",\"correct\":false},{\"text\":\"Inability to opt out\",\"correct\":false}]'></inline-check>\n\nQuantifying the asymmetry-of-access test makes it less abstract. A useful rough index is the **surveillance asymmetry score**: how much the institution sees relative to how much the learner can see, scaled by how many independent streams are combined and discounted by whether a real opt-out exists.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Surveillance asymmetry index (monitoring vs surveillance)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"streams\",\"label\":\"Independent data streams combined (keystroke, affect, gaze, social, temporal)\",\"min\":1,\"max\":5,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"streams\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"institution_visibility\",\"label\":\"Share the institution can see\",\"min\":0,\"max\":1,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"learner_visibility\",\"label\":\"Share the learner can see of their own profile\",\"min\":0.05,\"max\":1,\"step\":0.05,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":1},{\"name\":\"optout\",\"label\":\"Real opt-out available (1 = yes, 0 = no)\",\"min\":0,\"max\":1,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":1}]\n,\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Access asymmetry ratio (institution / learner)\",\"unit\":\"x\",\"expr\":\"institution_visibility / learner_visibility\"},{\"label\":\"Surveillance index (higher = more surveillance-like)\",\"unit\":\"\",\"expr\":\"streams * (institution_visibility / learner_visibility) * (2 - optout)\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"streams\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nSet the learner-visible share to its lowest, combine five streams, and remove the opt-out, and the surveillance index climbs steeply: that is the *Ogletree* corner, where deep aggregation, one-sided visibility, and a coerced \"consent\" all coincide. Drag the sliders back toward one stream, equal visibility, and a real opt-out, and the design slides back into bounded monitoring.\n\n---\n\n## Dark Patterns Specific to Learner-Facing AI\n\nSeveral engagement-optimization patterns are widely deployed in educational AI and are ethically distinct from their consumer counterparts because the audience includes minors whose self-regulation is still developing.\n\n| Dark pattern | Mechanism | Developmental vulnerability exploited |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Variable-ratio rewards | Streaks, surprise badges, unpredictable drops | Immature impulse control; slot-machine reinforcement |\n| Loss-aversion design | Streak-loss anxiety, lost leaderboard rank, progress shaming | Still-developing emotional self-regulation |\n| Manufactured social pressure | \"X classmates are working now,\" false peer presence, AI peer comments | Heightened adolescent peer-conformity sensitivity |\n| Engagement-optimized recommendation | Objective is time-on-platform, not learning | Cannot evaluate whether content serves their own goals |\n| Dark consent flows | Bundled consent, no granular control | Weak bargaining position; parents rarely read flows |\n\n**Variable-ratio reward schedules** mimic the reinforcement that makes slot machines effective. They reliably extend session length and shorten between-session intervals; their effect on actual learning is far less clear, and they exploit the limited self-regulation of younger learners.\n\n**Loss-aversion design** weaponizes a robust cognitive bias. Deploying it on minors who are still developing emotional self-regulation is hard to justify pedagogically.\n\n**Manufactured social pressure** uses live peer-presence cues and AI-generated peer comments. We previously catalogued ALGET's own AI peer-note injection as an example; it is not unique to this codebase, and the Norwegian Consumer Council's *Deceived by Design* documents many more.\n\n**Engagement-optimized content recommendations** drift toward content that holds attention regardless of learning value, by Goodhart's law: when time-on-platform becomes the target, it ceases to be a good measure of learning.\n\n**Dark consent flows** bundle product improvement, behavioral profiling, and required functionality with no granular control, inherited from consumer SaaS even though learners and parents have a weaker bargaining position.\n\nEach of these is **legal in most jurisdictions today**. Each is **ethically contested**. The EU Digital Services Act has begun to constrain some; US enforcement is uneven.\n\n<concept-diagram type=\"learner-autonomy-vs-engagement\"></concept-diagram>\n\n---\n\n## Learner Autonomy as Ethical Anchor\n\nSelf-determination theory (Deci and Ryan) frames human motivation around three needs: **autonomy** (volition over one's actions), **competence** (effective interaction with the environment), and **relatedness** (genuine connection with others). Educational AI that cultivates these supports learning. Educational AI that undermines them, particularly autonomy, produces compliance without development.\n\nA common misframe: \"engagement\" is treated as evidence of motivation, and motivation is treated as evidence of learning. Self-determination theory rejects this chain. Engagement that comes from manufactured loss aversion or variable-ratio reward is controlled motivation, not autonomous motivation, and the longitudinal evidence on the difference is unkind to the engagement-optimized products: when the manufactured cue is removed, the behavior collapses, because nothing internal was ever built.\n\nThe autonomy criterion does practical design work. It rules **out** persuasion mechanics that exploit limited self-regulation, surveillance the learner cannot inspect or decline, recommendation systems whose objective is platform-time, and consent flows the learner cannot meaningfully exercise. It rules **in** learner-visible records of what the system tracks, granular control over notifications and recommendations, pacing controls and self-directed pathways, and real exit ramps that do not penalize the learner.\n\nWe can make the SDT prediction concrete. Treat observed engagement as a sum of two parts: a durable **autonomous** component built from interest, plus a **controlled** component propped up by manufactured cues (streaks, loss aversion, manufactured peers). When the cue is removed, only the controlled part collapses, and how fast it collapses depends on how cue-dependent the design made the learner. Explore what survives.\n\n<parameter-explorer config='{\"title\":\"Engagement after the manufactured cue is removed (SDT)\",\"sliders\":[{\"name\":\"autonomous\",\"label\":\"Autonomous (interest-driven) engagement\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":30},{\"name\":\"controlled\",\"label\":\"Controlled (cue-driven) engagement at peak\",\"min\":0,\"max\":100,\"step\":5,\"unit\":\"%\",\"default\":50},{\"name\":\"weeks\",\"label\":\"Weeks since the cue (streak / leaderboard) was removed\",\"min\":0,\"max\":12,\"step\":1,\"unit\":\"wk\",\"default\":0},{\"name\":\"decay\",\"label\":\"Cue dependence (higher = faster collapse)\",\"min\":0.1,\"max\":1,\"step\":0.1,\"unit\":\"\",\"default\":0.5}],\"outputs\":[{\"label\":\"Surviving controlled engagement\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"controlled * (1 - decay) ^ weeks\"},{\"label\":\"Total engagement now\",\"unit\":\"%\",\"expr\":\"autonomous + controlled * (1 - decay) ^ weeks\"}],\"plot\":{\"x\":\"weeks\",\"output\":1}}'></parameter-explorer>\n\nPush the weeks slider out and watch the total fall toward the autonomous floor: the 40 percent lift a metric dashboard celebrates is mostly the controlled term, which SDT predicts evaporates once the streak or leaderboard disappears. Only the autonomous component is something the learner keeps.\n\n---\n\n## Worked Case Analysis: The Streaks-and-Leaderboards Redesign\n\n**Scenario.** A learning platform for high-school students introduces three features: daily streaks, leaderboards visible to the whole class, and AI-generated \"peer\" comments under each student's highlights. After one term, engagement metrics (daily active users, session length) improve 40 percent. The product team wants to ship the features to all schools. You are the instructional designer asked to sign off.\n\n**Step 1: Classify each feature against the surveillance tests and dark-pattern table.**\n\n| Feature | Dark pattern present? | SDT need threatened | Surveillance concern |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Daily streaks | Variable-ratio + loss aversion | Autonomy (controlled motivation) | Low (no new data stream) |\n| Class-visible leaderboard | Loss aversion + social pressure | Autonomy + relatedness (rivalry, shaming) | Medium (rank is a derived profile shown to peers) |\n| AI-generated peer comments | Manufactured social presence | Relatedness (fake connection) + autonomy | Medium (implies modeling of the learner) |\n\n**Step 2: Predict the long-run outcome using SDT.** The 40 percent engagement lift is controlled motivation. SDT predicts that once the streak or leaderboard cue is removed (summer break, a platform change, simple habituation), autonomous motivation and persistence will be *lower* than baseline, because the features substituted external pressure for internal interest. Engagement up does not imply learning up.\n\n**Step 3: Stakeholder and tradeoff table.**\n\n| Stakeholder | What they gain from shipping | What they lose |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Product team | Headline 40% engagement metric | Long-run retention risk; regulatory exposure |\n| Teacher | Short-term participation | Trust if shaming surfaces; managing leaderboard fallout |\n| High-performing student | Status from leaderboard | Anxiety to defend rank; extrinsic reframing of learning |\n| Struggling student | Little | Public shaming, loss-aversion stress, withdrawal |\n| Parent | Visible activity | Unconsented social profiling of their child |\n\n**Step 4: Decision.** Do not ship as designed. The struggling student bears concentrated harm for a metric that SDT predicts will not convert to learning. **Redesign:** keep streaks but make them private and self-set (autonomy-supportive goal-setting rather than imposed loss aversion); replace the class-visible leaderboard with private personal-best progress; remove AI-generated peer comments entirely, because fake relatedness is the least defensible mechanic of the three. Re-measure on a learning outcome, not engagement.\n\nThis is the move the autonomy anchor forces: a feature is not justified by the metric it lifts; it is justified by whether it builds something the learner keeps.\n\n---\n\n## Counterarguments and Limitations\n\nA serious analyst should hold the opposing case in view.\n\n**\"Some friction is good, and gamification demonstrably helps.\"** True in part. Well-designed gamification that supports competence (clear goals, informative feedback, optional challenge) is consistent with SDT. The line is not \"no game mechanics\" but \"no mechanics that substitute control for volition.\" A self-set streak is fine; a guilt-inducing streak-loss notification is not.\n\n**\"Engagement is a real prerequisite; a tool no one opens teaches nothing.\"** Also true. The autonomy critique does not say ignore engagement; it says do not treat engagement as the terminal goal or as a proxy for learning. Engagement is necessary, not sufficient, and must be earned with interest rather than manufactured with pressure.\n\n**\"The legal floor permits all of this.\"** Mostly correct today, and that is the limitation of a purely legal analysis. FERPA, COPPA, and IDEA set a floor, not a ceiling; most dark patterns are legal precisely because regulation lags design. The ethical bar is higher than the legal one, which is why a workflow that stops at \"is it legal?\" will ship harmful defaults.\n\n**\"Surveillance vs. monitoring is a spectrum, not a binary.\"** Correct, and the three tests are diagnostic, not a bright line. Their value is in forcing the specific questions (How deep is the aggregation? Can the learner see it? Can they decline it?) rather than in producing a single verdict.\n\n---\n\n## A Pre-Deployment Workflow for Educational AI\n\nCombining this module with the FERPA/COPPA/IDEA module from earlier:\n\n1. **Map data flows.** What is collected, by whom, retained how long, accessible to which roles?\n2. **Test for monitoring vs surveillance.** Aggregation depth, learner visibility, opt-out reality.\n3. **Audit engagement mechanics.** Are variable-ratio rewards, loss aversion, manufactured social pressure used? On minors?\n4. **Check learner autonomy.** Do learners have visibility into and control over what the system does about them?\n5. **Verify legal floor.** FERPA, COPPA, IDEA, GDPR (if applicable), DSA dark-pattern restrictions.\n6. **Verify procedural due process.** Algorithmic decisions affecting placement, services, or credentialing must satisfy IDEA-class oversight.\n7. **Document the audit trail.** A specific decision must be explainable to a parent or learner a year later.\n\nEducational AI is high-stakes. The workflow is intentionally heavy because the subjects are minors, the context is coercive, and the trajectories are durable.\n\n<dynamic-scenario topic=\"Educational AI Autonomy\" context=\"A learning platform for high-school students introduces daily streaks, leaderboards visible to classmates, and AI-generated peer comments under highlights. Engagement metrics improve 40%. Walk the autonomy-anchored audit: which dark patterns are present, what does self-determination theory predict about long-term motivation, and what redesigns would preserve learning value while removing the manipulative mechanics?\"></dynamic-scenario>\n\n<branching-scenario tree='{\"title\":\"Sign-off on the streaks-and-leaderboards release\",\"start\":\"s1\",\"nodes\":{\"s1\":{\"prompt\":\"Engagement is up 40 percent and the product team wants to ship daily streaks, class-visible leaderboards, and AI-generated peer comments to all schools. As the instructional designer, what do you do with the 40 percent number?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Approve; a 40 percent engagement lift is a strong result\",\"next\":\"ship\",\"feedback\":\"SDT warns this is controlled motivation. Engagement up does not imply learning up, and the lift is mostly the cue-dependent term.\"},{\"label\":\"Ask what the lift is made of and how it behaves when the cue is removed\",\"next\":\"diagnose\",\"feedback\":\"Right. Separate autonomous from controlled engagement before treating the metric as success.\"}]},\"ship\":{\"prompt\":\"After summer break the streaks reset and engagement falls below baseline; a struggling student who was publicly bottom of the leaderboard has disengaged entirely. What does this confirm?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"The lift was controlled motivation that collapsed without the cue, and the harm concentrated on the least powerful learner\",\"next\":\"lesson\",\"feedback\":\"Exactly the SDT prediction, and the stakeholder table flagged the struggling student as bearing concentrated harm.\"}]},\"diagnose\":{\"prompt\":\"The audit shows AI-generated peer comments simulate connection, the class leaderboard publicly shames low performers, and streaks use loss aversion. Which redesign best preserves learning value?\",\"choices\":[{\"label\":\"Keep all three but add a consent checkbox\",\"next\":\"weak\",\"feedback\":\"Consent does not cure fake relatedness or public shaming of minors. The mechanics themselves are the problem.\"},{\"label\":\"Make streaks private and self-set, replace the public leaderboard with personal-best progress, and remove AI peer comments entirely\",\"next\":\"redesign\",\"feedback\":\"This converts imposed loss aversion into autonomy-supportive goal-setting and removes the least defensible mechanic (fake relatedness).\"}]},\"weak\":{\"prompt\":\"Outcome: the manipulative mechanics ship behind a checkbox. The fake-relatedness and shaming harms remain, and the metric still will not convert to learning.\",\"feedback\":\"Reconsider: a feature is justified by what the learner keeps, not by a consent wrapper.\",\"choices\":[]},\"redesign\":{\"prompt\":\"Outcome: the design now supports autonomy and competence, and you commit to re-measuring on a learning outcome rather than engagement.\",\"feedback\":\"This is the move the autonomy anchor forces.\",\"choices\":[]},\"lesson\":{\"prompt\":\"Outcome: the metric that drove the decision was the wrong target (Goodhart), and the cost fell on the struggling learner. Next time, diagnose the engagement before shipping.\",\"feedback\":\"Justify features by what the learner keeps.\",\"choices\":[]}}}'></branching-scenario>\n\n<self-explain prompt=\"Using self-determination theory, explain why removing the AI-generated peer comments is described as the least defensible of the three mechanics to keep, even before measuring any outcome.\" answer=\"The peer comments manufacture relatedness, one of SDT's three basic needs, by faking genuine connection with others. Unlike a self-set streak, which can be reworked into autonomy-supportive goal-setting, a fabricated peer presence cannot be made authentic; it only ever produces controlled motivation and a false sense of relatedness. Because it substitutes a counterfeit for the real need rather than supporting it, it has no defensible learning rationale, so it is the first to remove.\"></self-explain>\n\n<concept-map concepts='[\"educational_surveillance\",\"learner_autonomy\",\"self_determination_theory\",\"engagement_dark_patterns\",\"manufactured_social_presence\"]'></concept-map>\n\n---\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- **Surveillance is not monitoring.** Use three tests: aggregation depth, asymmetry of access, inability to opt out. Choose bounded, learner-visible, declinable.\n- **Five dark patterns recur in learner-facing AI:** variable-ratio rewards, loss aversion, manufactured social pressure, engagement-optimized recommendation, dark consent flows. Each exploits a developmental vulnerability and is mostly legal but ethically contested.\n- **Engagement is not motivation, and motivation is not learning.** SDT predicts controlled motivation collapses when the manufactured cue is removed.\n- **Autonomy is the working anchor.** It rules out manipulation and surveillance; it rules in visibility, granular control, pacing, and real exit ramps.\n- **The legal floor is a floor.** A workflow that stops at \"is it legal?\" will ship harmful defaults; the ethical bar is higher.\n- **Justify features by what the learner keeps,** not by the metric they lift.\n\n---\n\n## What's Next?\n\nThe next module turns from defensive auditing to constructive design: how to build educational AI whose objective function is a *learning* outcome rather than an *engagement* proxy, and how to instrument that outcome so the autonomy-supportive choices made here can actually be measured rather than asserted.\n\n---\n\n## Check Your Understanding\n\n<interactive-quiz\n  question=\"A platform shows manufactured 'X classmates are working right now' notifications and AI-generated peer comments to drive engagement. Engagement metrics improve, but self-determination theory predicts what longer-term effect on learner motivation?\"\n  options='[{\"text\":\"Stronger autonomous motivation, since engagement is up.\",\"isCorrect\":false},{\"text\":\"Engagement up does not imply autonomous motivation up; manufactured social pressure and AI-as-peer cultivate compliance and dependence rather than autonomy. The longitudinal SDT evidence predicts weaker autonomous motivation and worse persistence once the manipulative mechanics are removed.\",\"isCorrect\":true},{\"text\":\"No effect on motivation either way.\",\"isCorrect\":false}]'\n  explanation=\"Correct. Engagement is not a proxy for autonomous motivation in SDT. Manufactured pressure produces compliance, which collapses without the manufactured cue.\"></interactive-quiz>\n\n## References\n\n- Tabassi, E. (2023). *Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0).* NIST AI 100-1. https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.100-1\n- UNESCO. (2021). *Recommendation on the Ethics of Artificial Intelligence.* https://www.unesco.org/en/legal-affairs/recommendation-ethics-artificial-intelligence?hub=1063\n- European Parliament and Council of the European Union. (2024). *Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 (Artificial Intelligence Act).* https://eur-lex.europa.eu/legal-content/EN/TXT/?uri=celex%3A32024R1689\n","simulation":null,"illustration":null,"practice":{"problems":[{"id":"edu_monitor_vs_surveil","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"educational_surveillance","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["educational_surveillance"],"stem":"Which set of properties distinguishes educational surveillance from formative monitoring?","options":["Cost, color scheme, vendor reputation.","Aggregation depth (combined streams beyond purpose), asymmetry of access (institution sees, learner does not), and inability to opt out without leaving the institution.","Number of users.","Whether AI is used."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"These three properties capture the line. Defensible educational AI chooses bounded scope, learner-visible records, and real opt-out for non-essential streams.","misconception_id":"all_recording_is_monitoring","learning_objective_ids":["ai-ethics-06-02-lo-01"]},{"id":"edu_engagement_motivation","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"self_determination_theory","difficulty":"hard","concept_ids":["self_determination_theory","manufactured_social_presence"],"stem":"A learning platform's engagement metrics rise 40% after adding streaks and manufactured peer presence. What does self-determination theory (Deci & Ryan) predict about autonomous motivation?","options":["Autonomous motivation rises in proportion to engagement.","Engagement rising does not imply autonomous motivation rising. Manufactured social pressure and variable-ratio rewards cultivate compliance and dependence; SDT predicts weaker autonomous motivation and worse persistence once manipulative mechanics are removed.","No effect either way.","Engagement is the same as motivation by definition."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"SDT distinguishes autonomous motivation (volitional engagement aligned with values) from controlled motivation (compliance under pressure). Engagement metrics conflate them.","misconception_id":"engagement_is_motivation","learning_objective_ids":["ai-ethics-06-02-lo-03"]},{"id":"edu_dark_pattern_recognize","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"engagement_dark_patterns","difficulty":"medium","concept_ids":["engagement_dark_patterns"],"stem":"Which engagement mechanism most directly imports slot-machine reinforcement schedules into educational AI?","options":["Daily quiz at fixed time.","Variable-ratio reward schedules: streaks, surprise rewards, unpredictable badge drops. They extend session length and shorten between-session intervals while exploiting limited self-regulation in younger learners.","Static progress bar.","Read-only transcript."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Variable-ratio reinforcement is the slot-machine pattern. Its deployment on minors is hard to justify pedagogically; learning impact is far less clear than session-length impact.","misconception_id":"gamification_is_always_pedagogy","learning_objective_ids":["ai-ethics-06-02-lo-02"]},{"id":"edu_autonomy_design","type":"multiple_choice","concept_id":"learner_autonomy","difficulty":"easy","concept_ids":["learner_autonomy","self_determination_theory"],"stem":"Which design move most directly respects learner autonomy under SDT?","options":["Hide the system's data collection from learners.","Make the system's records about the learner visible to the learner; provide granular control over notifications and recommendations; offer real exit ramps that do not penalize the learner.","Increase the streak penalty.","Add more leaderboards."],"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Autonomy under SDT requires volition. Learner-visible records, granular control, and real exit ramps make volition real rather than nominal.","misconception_id":"autonomy_is_no_design","learning_objective_ids":["ai-ethics-06-02-lo-03","ai-ethics-06-02-lo-04"]}]},"misconceptions":[{"id":"all_recording_is_monitoring","pattern":"surveillance_minimization","description":"Treating any data recording as 'just monitoring' regardless of aggregation depth, learner visibility, or opt-out reality","trigger":"learner labels webcam emotion detection as 'engagement monitoring'","feedback":"Aggregation depth, asymmetry of access, and inability to opt out distinguish surveillance from monitoring. Bounded, learner-visible, opt-out-real records are monitoring; combined-stream, learner-blind, mandatory records are surveillance.","rail_action":"explain"},{"id":"engagement_is_motivation","pattern":"metric_conflation","description":"Treating engagement metrics as evidence of autonomous motivation","trigger":"learner cites session-length increase as proof of motivated learning","feedback":"SDT distinguishes autonomous motivation from controlled motivation. Manufactured engagement produces controlled motivation that collapses without the manipulative cue.","rail_action":"represent"},{"id":"gamification_is_always_pedagogy","pattern":"design_pattern_legitimization","description":"Treating gamification mechanics as automatically pedagogical","trigger":"learner adds streaks and badges and labels the change 'gamification for learning'","feedback":"Variable-ratio reinforcement borrows from slot-machine design. Some gamification supports learning; some exploits limited self-regulation. The distinction requires explicit pedagogical justification, not default deployment.","rail_action":"practice"},{"id":"autonomy_is_no_design","pattern":"autonomy_misframe","description":"Equating respect for learner autonomy with design abstinence ('do nothing')","trigger":"learner argues autonomy means refusing to scaffold or recommend at all","feedback":"Autonomy means volitional control. Design supports autonomy by surfacing what the system does, giving granular control, and making exit real. Active design is consistent with autonomy; manipulative or hidden design is not.","rail_action":"ask"}],"content_version":{"content_version":"dd38923adef7c92da6ee0422cc2155829cf93175b3e752e3926955ebce991f93","algorithm":"sha256-mdx+meta+practice+misconceptions-v1","parts":["mdx","meta","practice","misconceptions"]}}